@meta
id: 02-05
title: u is the key you are missing
chapter: 02
type: lesson
skills: undo
par: 97
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl+Z
open: CHANGELOG.md

@brief
So far you can add text and you can save. You cannot remove anything —
the delete operators are three chapters away. That sounds like a hole,
and for one keystroke it is not: `u` undoes the last change, and until
chapter 8 it is the only eraser you have.

The changelog needs a note about audit logging. Do this in order, and do
the wrong step deliberately — it is the lesson.

  1. Put the cursor on the 2.13 bullet, the last line of the file, and
     append to it:

         and are written to the audit log

     so the line reads

         - Orders can be cancelled before they ship and are written to the audit log

  2. Wrong line. Audit logging shipped in 2.14, not 2.13. Undo it.

  3. Put the same text on the 2.14 bullet instead, and save.

Undo has to give you back the 2.13 line exactly as it was, including the
space before `and`. You have no way to fix it by hand if it does not.

@keys
| `u` | undo the last change |
| `:undo` | the same thing, spelled out |

@hints
`u` is pressed in normal mode, so `Esc` out of insert first. In insert
mode it just types the letter u.
--
One `u` undoes the whole append, not one character of it. Everything you
typed between `a` and `Esc` is a single change.
--
After the undo the cursor is sitting on the line that changed back, which
is the 2.13 bullet. The 2.14 bullet is three rows up: `kkk`.

@solution
    jjjjjj $ a  and are written to the audit log <Esc>
    u
    kkk $ a  and are written to the audit log <Esc>
    ZZ

One keystroke removed thirty-three characters cleanly. That is why vim
users make a mess without worrying: the cost of being wrong is `u`.

Two details that pay off later. Vim's undo is per buffer and lives in
memory, so every file you have open has its own independent history.
And `u` is not a fixed number of undos — vim keeps a thousand by default,
so "undo as far back as you like" is close enough to true.

Do not confuse `u` with `:q!`. `:q!` throws away everything since the
last write, all at once, and ends the session. `u` walks back one change
at a time and you keep editing. In this lesson `:q!` would have cost you
the correct edit too, because you had not written it yet.

@start CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## 2.14
- Refunds now respect the thirty day window

## 2.13
- Orders can be cancelled before they ship

@expected CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## 2.14
- Refunds now respect the thirty day window and are written to the audit log

## 2.13
- Orders can be cancelled before they ship
